__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.env
.DS_Store
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# No trailing slash, on purpose. `.venv/` matches a DIRECTORY only, and a symlink is a
# file to git -- so `ln -s ../shared/.venv .venv`, which is a reasonable thing to do in a
# worktree, produced an untracked entry that `git add -A` would have committed as an
# absolute path pointing at one machine. Caught here before it shipped.
.venv
venv

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- data/
#
# data/ splits in two, and the split is the whole durability story of this repo.
#
# THE DATABASE IS NOT COMMITTED. Two independent reasons, either sufficient:
#
#   It contains umich_sca. The University of Michigan grants permission-free USE of
#   its public Surveys of Consumers tables and separately prohibits redistribution
#   without written consent (faq.php vs agreement.php -- both quoted in
#   sources/umich.py). Charting them is fine; mirroring them into a public repo is
#   not. So the file is fetched and read like any other, and never committed.
#
#   It does not need committing. Nine of the ten sources are backfillable=True, so
#   losing this file costs one `make backfill`. It is a cache of public APIs.
#
# data/snapshots/ IS COMMITTED, and nothing below may ignore it. The tenth source
# (openrouter_pricing) serves only "today" with no archive, so a missed run is a
# permanent hole and its history exists solely because we keep it. It lives in git as
# one immutable CSV per snapshot date. tests/test_sources.py asserts both directions
# of this via `git check-ignore` -- that the database is ignored and that
# data/snapshots/ is NOT -- because a comment cannot catch either mistake.
#
# The pattern describes the SHAPE of a store, not the default path, because every
# script takes `--db` (scripts/ingest.py, scripts/build.py, scripts/status.py). An
# earlier `data/*.sqlite` matched one directory level and one extension, so a store
# written to `data/offchart.db`, `data/offchart.sqlite3`, `data/sub/offchart.sqlite`
# or a repo-root `scratch.sqlite` held every restricted row and was committable with
# the whole suite green. Ignoring by extension anywhere in the tree is the version
# that matches the rule: a store is defined by what it contains, not by its path.
*.sqlite*
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# ...with one deliberate exception: the corpus the suite runs against. It is safe to
# commit only because its restricted slice is FABRICATED rather than sampled -- see
# tests/fixtures/build.py. tests/test_sources.py asserts this file stays committable,
# so closing the hole above cannot silently un-commit the fixture.
!tests/fixtures/fixture.sqlite

# The parquet store this repo used until the SQLite migration. Ignored rather than
# merely deleted: `make backfill` no longer writes these, but a checkout of an older
# commit does, and a stray 115 MB of them getting re-committed is a silent mistake.
data/*.parquet

# Boards that were built and then set aside. Kept on disk so they are cheap to
# revive, out of the tree so the repo shows only what ships. They are still in
# git history: `git show 42aa2dd:panels/_parked/screen.py` recovers any of them.
.parked/

# Claude Code's working directory: ignore everything in it EXCEPT the settings we
# want shared.
#
# The pattern is `.claude/*` and NOT `.claude/`, and that is load-bearing rather
# than stylistic. Git will not re-include a file whose parent directory has been
# excluded by a directory pattern -- it never descends into an excluded directory to
# find the exception. So `.claude/` followed by `!.claude/settings.local.json`
# silently does nothing and the file stays ignored while the .gitignore reads as
# though it were tracked. Excluding the CONTENTS (`.claude/*`) leaves the directory
# itself visible, which is what makes the negation bite.
#
# What the blanket rule is really for is worktrees/: `git worktree add` puts linked
# checkouts there, each a full copy of the tree including data/, gigabytes at the
# time of writing. Each also contains a `.git` FILE, so git sees a nested repository
# and `git add -A` either warns and skips or writes a gitlink -- both awkward to undo.
.claude/*
!.claude/settings.local.json
